Z||000000||DjKid||08-30-2000||11:44 AM||||whats a good price for a used LT1 vette engine? would 750$ be sweet or no?<BR><P>------------------<BR>DjKid - 88 Coupe<BR>'Blue Knight"||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||08-30-2000||12:03 PM||||Depends on the mileage and completeness. If it's a complete with all accessories, harness and ECM and less than 150k, sure.<P>If it's a long block, it's not worth that for a swap, only as a replacement engine. The cost to make it usable will be very high.<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000002||RDosdorian||08-30-2000||08:42 PM||||TerryK is right, it depends on the specifics of the motor.  I will tell you what I spent for a complete LT1.<BR>The specifics are 1995 LT1 from a Corvette.  All the accessories, incl. harness, fuel rail covers, ecm, etc.  Engine had 23,000 on it.  I paid $3,000.  Some might say this is high.  All I can say is I live in Northern California and everything is high.  I, however, am happy with what i got for the price I paid.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.goldengatefieros.org/RDosdorian_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Boomtastic||08-30-2000||09:00 PM||||The allure of the "LT1" name on an engine isn't really anything spectacular. That designation is more geared towards the upper half of the motor (injection, heads, etc). The lower half is simply a 1 piece rear main seal, 4 bolt block. So, with that said and done, *any* 4 bolt block from '87 and up can be made into an LT1 motor. (Of course, I could go off on a tangent and say that I prefer a 2 bolt block over the 4 bolt, since there is more "meat" to add the splayed main bearing caps .. but I digress.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">) Either way - a well "seasoned" block is what you want. Mileage isn't a main concern if you are planning on rebuilding it and all surfaces are "square". I used to sell bare blocks to machine shops all the time. They would immediately throw the block outside into the weater to let it season. This expanding/contracting of the block allows it to "settle", much like a house does. Of course, this is all relevant if you are rebuilding the engine. If you are simply planning on buying and dropping it in, well then go for the lowest mileage and lowest price. In either case, make sure you can spin the engine over at least 1 complete revolution.||||reg||1
Z||000004||webbee||08-30-2000||09:13 PM||||The seasoning gimmick is also true for brake drums/rotors, too. The majors used to do it in the 50/60's but no more.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||08-30-2000||11:27 PM||||$3000 complete sounds about right for way over here too (Sacramento).<P>Aren't the LT1's shorter (length-wise) than the older TPI's from the late 80's?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Boomtastic||08-31-2000||08:30 AM||||The LT1 engines have a shorter height and length to the upper intake plenum, revese flow cooling system, OptiSpark system, dry throttle body (no coolant piped in them), and a host of other "wierd" things, but they are basically built around the standard 350 block. My point, which I muddled up myself  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">, was that a standard late model 350 with any form of fuel injection (TPI, LT1, or LS-1 style) is still going to kick some butt. The term LT1, while synonomous &lt;sp&gt; with 'power', is not be "the end all, be all" of motors. Because the LT-1 was only used for approx. 5 years, it may be more feasible (read "cost effective") to look into other options, such as grabbing a bare block and building up your own monster.||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||08-31-2000||09:44 AM||||Yeah agree, but it's just not feasable out here in CA. Such is life.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Archie||08-31-2000||10:53 AM||||I just picked up a couple of new LT1's, I call them the Twins right now. I could be talked into selling one of them for the right price.<P>Archie<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-616s.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||1
